    "evidence_text": "HSwMS Gustav V\n\n However, the ship remained at Berga Academy of War as of 1968.\n\nVickers machine gun\n\nHMS James Watt\n\n She was the only Royal Navy ship to bear this name.\n\nThe Royal African Rifles\n\nIn August 1914 a consignment of Vickers machine guns are stolen off a Royal Navy ship, HMS \"Marlin\".\n\nHistory of the Sri Lanka Navy\n\n It continued under RN operational and administrative command until March 1946.\n\n All were manned exclusively by CRNVR personnel.\n\nHMS Valiant (1863)\n\n Her builders went bankrupt shortly after she was laid down, which significantly delayed her completion.\n\nCapture of the Vigilant\n\n Captain Douglas in the Mermaid (40 guns) engaged the French ship Vigilant.\n\n Captain Durell was next to give a broadside.\n\n The Commodore got alongside the ship they fired briskly, tearing the rigging and sails to pieces.\n\n Fog settled in and the Vigilant got away.\n\n They took 100 French sailors prisoner to Boston.\n\nHMS Unicorn (1748)\n\n She was originally ordered as a 24-gun ship to the draft of the French privateer \"Tyger\".\n\nHMS Hannibal (1896)\n\n The ship had a top speed of 16 kn .\n\nHMS Benbow (1913)",
